  "vulnerability_information": "curl 8.21.0 case-folds the entire TLS session-cache peer key, so two distinct CA files whose Unix paths differ only by case can share a TLS session ticket.\n\nAt `lib/vtls/vtls_scache.c:723`, `curl_strequal()` compares keys that include case-sensitive CA, CRL, and issuer paths, even though `docs/internals/TLS-SESSIONS.md` says lookup uses exactly the same peer key.\n\nRun the attached `poc-casefolded-scache.py`: each of its two vulnerable passes first trusts `Trust.pem`, switches to an unrelated `trust.pem`, logs `SSL reusing session`, sends `Authorization: Bearer VICTIM_CANARY`, receives two HTTP 200 responses, and exits 0.\n\n    cert controls: good_rc=0 bad_rc=2\n    run1 case-only bad CA: rc=0 reused=True canary_sent=True http_200=2\n    run2 case-only bad CA: rc=0 reused=True canary_sent=True http_200=2\n    control different-name bad CA: rc=60 reused=False canary_sent=False http_200=1\n\nThe control uses the same unrelated CA under `Different.pem`; curl performs a full certificate handshake, exits 60, and never sends the canary, while changing the known-key lookup to `strcmp()` produces the same safe result.\n\nCurrent master `2687751471c04e07a9c5da78338569d97aff7fd4` is still affected; this is a session-ticket-cache regression distinct from CVE-2021-22924's live connection-cache bug because the vulnerable cache was added later by `fa0ccd9f`, and I used AI to assist the audit and report while personally rerunning and checking every claim.\n\n## Impact\n\nAn application that reuses a multi handle or shared TLS session cache across per-transfer CA profiles can send credentials or request data to a server that the active CA profile explicitly does not trust.",
